---
title: 'Template: How to Write a Learn Article'
description: >-
  Copy this file, rename it to your-article-slug.mdoc, and replace every field.
  This description becomes the meta description and link preview, so keep it
  around 155 characters and make it read like a sentence.
pubDate: 2026-06-15
# updatedDate: 2026-07-01   # uncomment when you revise a published piece
# author:                 # confirmed writer; leave absent until known
# authorUrl:              # complete http:// or https:// URL, optional
editorialStatus: article  # article or research-note; use draft to hide either
field: Creation Stories
tags:
  - creation
# cover:                  # optional existing media ID
# coverImage:             # path from a real editor upload; takes priority over cover
# coverAlt:               # describe what is visible
# coverCaption:           # identify the actual artwork or process shown
# coverCredit:            # confirmed creator/photographer and required licence credit
# sources:
#   - title:              # author, title, publication, date, relevant page numbers
#     url:                # complete http:// or https:// URL; optional for print sources
# relatedCard: 1            # 1–64, optional. Links the article to a deck card.
draft: true
---

Replace these instructions with your article. Start with the particular work,
experience, or question the reader is here to explore. Use only confirmed
details and your own account; do not invent a title, date, attribution, or
creative history to fill a gap. The body uses Markdoc, with familiar Markdown
formatting.

## A structure for a creation story

Introduce the actual work and its confirmed title, medium, dimensions, and
date where relevant. Describe the starting point, then walk through the
materials, sketches, decisions, and revisions that shaped the piece. Use
process photographs and details to show what changed. Reflect on what you
discovered and what remains unresolved.

These are prompts rather than required sections. Change the field if the
article is about research or a tradition instead. There is no minimum word
count. Develop the subject as fully as the piece needs.

## Artwork, details, and sources

Upload the actual cover artwork in the sidebar. Add alternative text,
a caption identifying what is shown, and confirmed credits. For process
photographs, use the body's image control. Put each image in its own paragraph
and fill in Caption and credit to display a caption below it.

The text-editor syntax is shown below; replace every placeholder with the
details and path of a real image before using it:

```markdown
![Description of what is visible](/learn/images/articles/your-article/detail.jpg "Confirmed caption and credit")
```

Use the Sources and further reading fields for references. Include the author,
title, publication, date, and relevant page numbers; a URL is optional for print
sources. Link evidence near historical or factual claims in the body, and
distinguish those claims from your own interpretation.

## Use H2 for the main sections

Headings start at level 2 (`##`) because the article title is already the H1.
Keep sections short and let each one carry a single idea. Sub-points can go a
level deeper with `###` when a section genuinely needs it.

Paragraphs are just blank-line-separated text. *Italic* and **bold** both work.
Links look like [this](https://mandalacodes.com/universal-language).

### Lists, when a section needs structure

- A bullet for each parallel point.
- Keep them short; if a bullet runs to three lines, it probably wants to be a
  paragraph instead.
- **Lead term.** A bold lead and a short gloss is a clean way to define things,
  matching the house style in the mandala article. (No em dashes anywhere on
  this site: use a comma, a period, a colon, or "to" for ranges.)

Numbered steps work when order matters:

1. First the reader does this.
2. Then this.
3. The payoff lands here.

## Quotes and emphasis

> A blockquote sets apart a line worth pausing on: a definition, a saying, or
> a sentence you want to slow the reader down for.

## Closing

End by pointing somewhere. If the piece relates to a specific card, set
`relatedCard` in the frontmatter above and the footer link to that card renders
automatically. Otherwise close with an invitation, the way the mandala article
ends by inviting the reader to draw a card.

